    (
        23,
        1,
        "c",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Psalms</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>Cyril reads &#x27;The Lord is my shepherd&#x27; Christologically: the shepherd of Psalm 23 is the same as the Good Shepherd of John 10, the same as the Lamb of Revelation. The &#x27;I shall not want&#x27; is a confession of complete dependence on the Incarnate Word who is at once Lamb and Shepherd. Tewahedo liturgy preserves this Christological reading prominently in the burial office.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, on Psalm 22/23 (LXX numbering). NPNF Series 2, vol. 7. PD.",
    ),
    (
        23,
        1,
        "d",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Athanasius of Alexandria",
        "Athanasius of Alexandria (350).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Athanasius of Alexandria</strong> <em>Epistola ad Marcellinum §3</em> <small>(c. 350 AD)</small><p>&#x27;The LORD is my shepherd; I shall not want&#x27; (LXX 22:1 — kyrios poimainei me kai ouden me hysterēsei) — Athanasius marks Ps 23 (LXX 22) as the PARADIGMATIC PASTORAL-COMFORT-PSALM in the Letter to Marcellinus: prescribed for the soul-tending-toward-providential-trust. The kyrios poimainei me (the Lord shepherds-me) is read Christologically — the Word-incarnate is the Johannine ho poimēn ho kalos (the Good Shepherd, Jn 10:11). The Athanasian pastoral-spiritual hermeneutic transforms the Psalter into a Christic-prayer-book: each Psalm voices a stance-toward-Christ that the praying-soul takes-up. The Tewahedo Säʿatat (Liturgy of the Hours) Psalter-pattern preserves this Athanasian pastoral-Psalter use.</p></aside>',
        "Athanasius of Alexandria, Epistola ad Marcellinum de Interpretatione Psalmorum (Letter to Marcellinus on the Interpretation of the Psalms), in Select Writings and Letters of Athanasius, Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers (NPNF), Series 2, Vol. 4, ed. Archibald Robertson (Oxford / T&T Clark, 1892). PD. Greek text in Migne PG 27 (1857). The Letter is Athanasius's principal pastoral-spiritual work — a personal letter to Marcellinus (recovering from illness) explaining how to use each Psalm as prayer for specific spiritual circumstances. Hermeneutic anchor for the Tewahedo Office (Säʿatat) Psalter-recitation tradition.",
    ),
