    (
        45,
        6,
        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Athanasius of Alexandria",
        "Athanasius of Alexandria (350).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Athanasius of Alexandria</strong> <em>Orationes contra Arianos II.13</em> <small>(c. 350 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Thy throne, O God, is for ever and ever: the sceptre of thy kingdom is a right sceptre&#x27; (LXX 44:7 — ho thronos sou ho theos eis ton aiōna tou aiōnos) — Athanasius marks Ps 45:6 (LXX 44:7) as the DIRECT VOCATIVE-ADDRESS of the Son as ho theos (the-God, with definite article). The Father is the speaker; the Son the addressee; the address is unambiguous in the vocative-with-article. The author of Hebrews (1:8) cites this verse against the angels — &#x27;unto which of the angels said he at any time...&#x27; (1:5) and culminates with this &#x27;But unto the Son he saith, Thy throne, O God&#x27; — Athanasius treats Heb 1:8&#x27;s citation as the canonical apostolic-warrant for reading Ps 45:6&#x27;s vocative as direct-Trinitarian-address, not metaphor or hypostatized-attribute.</p></aside>',
        "Athanasius of Alexandria, Orationes contra Arianos (Four Discourses Against the Arians, I-IV), in Select Writings and Letters of Athanasius, Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers (NPNF), Series 2, Vol. 4, ed. Archibald Robertson (Oxford / T&T Clark, 1892). PD. Greek text in Migne PG 26 (1857).",
    ),
