    (
        119,
        11,
        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Athanasius of Alexandria",
        "Athanasius of Alexandria (350).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Athanasius of Alexandria</strong> <em>Epistola ad Marcellinum §30</em> <small>(c. 350 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Thy word have I hid in mine heart, that I might not sin against thee&#x27; (LXX 118:11 — en tē kardia mou ekrypsa ta logia sou hopōs an mē hamartō soi) — Athanasius marks Ps 119 (LXX 118) — the longest-Psalm, structured by the Hebrew-alphabet — as the WORD-INTERNALIZATION PARADIGM in the Letter to Marcellinus. The praying-soul does not merely-recite-the-word but HIDES-IT-IN-THE-HEART (ekrypsa en tē kardia) as protective-talisman-against-sin. The logia (oracles/divine-utterances) are Spirit-breathed-Scripture, and their-hiding-in-the-heart is the Spirit&#x27;s-work-of-sanctifying-memory. Athanasius&#x27;s pastoral-instruction: memorize the Psalter completely; the soul that knows-the-Psalter-by-heart has the Spirit&#x27;s-prayer-vocabulary always-at-hand.</p></aside>',
        "Athanasius of Alexandria, Epistola ad Marcellinum de Interpretatione Psalmorum (Letter to Marcellinus on the Interpretation of the Psalms), in Select Writings and Letters of Athanasius, Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers (NPNF), Series 2, Vol. 4, ed. Archibald Robertson (Oxford / T&T Clark, 1892). PD. Greek text in Migne PG 27 (1857). The Letter is Athanasius's principal pastoral-spiritual work — a personal letter to Marcellinus (recovering from illness) explaining how to use each Psalm as prayer for specific spiritual circumstances. Hermeneutic anchor for the Tewahedo Office (Säʿatat) Psalter-recitation tradition.",
    ),
